WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.271 --> 00:00:04.104
- What's up Believe Nation?

00:00:04.104 --> 00:00:05.437
My name is Evan Carmichael.

00:00:05.437 --> 00:00:08.315
My one word is believe and
I believe that entrepreneurs

00:00:08.315 --> 00:00:11.553
are going to solve all of
the world's major problems.

00:00:11.553 --> 00:00:13.975
I started the Did You Know
series to try to learn

00:00:13.975 --> 00:00:16.338
a little bit more about some
of our famous entrepreneurs

00:00:16.338 --> 00:00:18.504
that we love and look up
to and try to give you

00:00:18.504 --> 00:00:21.546
some new facts and little
behind-the-scenes things

00:00:21.546 --> 00:00:23.256
that maybe you didn't know before.

00:00:23.256 --> 00:00:26.138
And, so today we're going to
learn nine surprising facts

00:00:26.138 --> 00:00:28.165
about Warren Buffet.

00:00:28.165 --> 00:00:30.748
(lively music)

00:00:38.781 --> 00:00:39.871
(bell ringing)

00:00:39.871 --> 00:00:42.483
Fact number one, he bought his first stock

00:00:42.483 --> 00:00:44.681
when he was 11 years old.

00:00:44.681 --> 00:00:48.431
- I got my first stock
in the spring of 1942.

00:00:49.873 --> 00:00:54.040
I was 11 and if you remember,
you don't remember (chuckles).

00:00:55.131 --> 00:00:56.567
No one watching this will remember,

00:00:56.567 --> 00:00:59.565
but we were losing the war at that time.

00:00:59.565 --> 00:01:02.232
We were getting, we were getting

00:01:03.232 --> 00:01:05.473
totally creamed in the South Pacific

00:01:05.473 --> 00:01:08.723
and the Philippines fell and
the death march at Baatan,

00:01:08.723 --> 00:01:10.010
and all that sort of the thing.

00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:11.467
It was not until the battle of Midway,

00:01:11.467 --> 00:01:12.300
which was a little later,

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:13.816
that things started turning around.

00:01:13.816 --> 00:01:16.542
But, I was optimistic on the country then.

00:01:16.542 --> 00:01:18.809
And, I've been optimistic on
the country ever since then.

00:01:18.809 --> 00:01:20.857
And, incidentally, the Dow
Jones Average then was 100

00:01:20.857 --> 00:01:24.402
and it's 17,000 now (chuckles).

00:01:24.402 --> 00:01:28.371
No one has ever been a success
betting against America

00:01:28.371 --> 00:01:31.135
since 1776 and they're not going
to be a success in the future

00:01:31.135 --> 00:01:31.968
doing it either.

00:01:31.968 --> 00:01:33.244
(numbers rattling)
(bell ringing)

00:01:33.244 --> 00:01:36.293
- Fact number two, he was
rejected by Harvard University

00:01:36.293 --> 00:01:39.530
and subsequently went to
Columbia where he mentored

00:01:39.530 --> 00:01:41.626
under Benjamin Graham.

00:01:41.626 --> 00:01:43.833
- So, when you graduated
from University of Nebraska,

00:01:43.833 --> 00:01:47.766
you applied to Harvard Business
School and were rejected.

00:01:47.766 --> 00:01:49.356
- [Warren] That's true.

00:01:49.356 --> 00:01:51.501
- Has Harvard ever announced that they've

00:01:51.501 --> 00:01:52.334
regretted that decision?

00:01:52.334 --> 00:01:55.214
(audience laughing)

00:01:55.214 --> 00:01:56.777
- I understand the Development Officer's

00:01:56.777 --> 00:01:58.321
kind of unhappy (laughs).

00:01:58.321 --> 00:02:02.488
(audience laughing)
(audience applauding)

00:02:04.775 --> 00:02:07.115
- So, you rejected by Harvard, you went to

00:02:07.115 --> 00:02:09.248
Columbia Business School
and then you met the man

00:02:09.248 --> 00:02:11.889
who you really idolized, Ben Graham.

00:02:11.889 --> 00:02:14.434
And, when you took his
class, was he as great

00:02:14.434 --> 00:02:15.683
as you had thought he was going to be?

00:02:15.683 --> 00:02:16.592
- He really was.

00:02:16.592 --> 00:02:19.735
He only taught one semester, one class.

00:02:19.735 --> 00:02:23.179
And, he came up from Wall
Street and taught this class.

00:02:23.179 --> 00:02:24.846
That's the reason I went to Columbia.

00:02:24.846 --> 00:02:29.023
And, I knew what he, I'd
already read all the books

00:02:29.023 --> 00:02:31.112
and everything, but it was inspirational

00:02:31.112 --> 00:02:34.185
just to be around him and
it made a real difference

00:02:34.185 --> 00:02:35.268
in my life.

00:02:35.268 --> 00:02:36.610
(numbers rattling)
(bell ringing)

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:40.968
- Fact number three, he reads
five to six hours a day.

00:02:40.968 --> 00:02:44.410
- I should mention one
thing about reading,

00:02:44.410 --> 00:02:47.485
it was at the library here
at Columbia that at which

00:02:47.485 --> 00:02:51.439
I spent probably more time
than any other student.

00:02:51.439 --> 00:02:53.374
I lived there practically.

00:02:53.374 --> 00:02:56.314
But, you know I pulled a book out there,

00:02:56.314 --> 00:02:57.587
happened to be Who's Who in America,

00:02:57.587 --> 00:02:59.584
and it told me something
about my professor,

00:02:59.584 --> 00:03:01.034
Benjamin Graham, and then I looked up,

00:03:01.034 --> 00:03:03.478
I went to the library and
I said I want to learn more

00:03:03.478 --> 00:03:06.064
about this because I
learned this over here,

00:03:06.064 --> 00:03:07.325
that changed my whole life.

00:03:07.325 --> 00:03:11.492
We own GEICO now because of
that librarian directing me

00:03:12.372 --> 00:03:16.240
to some other book and then
following through on that.

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:18.657
It's the chance, I read about

00:03:19.548 --> 00:03:22.055
1/5 the pace that Bill does,

00:03:22.055 --> 00:03:24.462
but I still spend five or
six hours a day reading.

00:03:24.462 --> 00:03:26.055
I mean it just, you can learn so much.

00:03:26.055 --> 00:03:27.616
I particularly love biography.

00:03:27.616 --> 00:03:30.366
Just to be able to live the lives

00:03:31.709 --> 00:03:33.884
of these people that have been,

00:03:33.884 --> 00:03:37.118
that seem so extraordinary
and the lessons,

00:03:37.118 --> 00:03:39.471
the discouragements they faced,
just everything about it.

00:03:39.471 --> 00:03:41.620
So, you can't get enough of reading.

00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:42.800
(numbers rattling)
(bell ringing)

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:46.937
- Fact number four, he plays
bridge 12 hours a week.

00:03:46.937 --> 00:03:50.230
- If I'm playing bridge
and a naked woman walks by

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:53.354
I don't even see her (chuckles).

00:03:53.354 --> 00:03:56.131
Don't test me on that (chuckles).

00:03:56.131 --> 00:03:59.804
- [Host] When he's not counting
money, he's counting cards.

00:03:59.804 --> 00:04:03.333
Buffett plays bridge
about 12 hours a week.

00:04:03.333 --> 00:04:06.997
- I one time said that I
wouldn't mind going to jail

00:04:06.997 --> 00:04:09.231
if I had the right three cellmates

00:04:09.231 --> 00:04:10.485
so we could play bridge all the time.

00:04:10.485 --> 00:04:12.049
In fact, I might even prefer it.

00:04:12.049 --> 00:04:13.805
- Are there lessons for business,

00:04:13.805 --> 00:04:15.140
for later life in this game?

00:04:15.140 --> 00:04:16.394
- Well there's a lot of lessons in it.

00:04:16.394 --> 00:04:18.570
You have to look at all the facts.

00:04:18.570 --> 00:04:21.718
You have to draw inferences
from what you've heard,

00:04:21.718 --> 00:04:23.135
what you've seen.

00:04:24.229 --> 00:04:27.214
You have to discard improper theories

00:04:27.214 --> 00:04:30.267
about what the hand has as more
evidence comes in sometimes.

00:04:30.267 --> 00:04:34.161
You have to work with your
partner particularly on defense.

00:04:34.161 --> 00:04:36.332
- [Host] Not to say that
bridge will ever be as big

00:04:36.332 --> 00:04:40.328
as it once was, but you can
do worse than heeding advice

00:04:40.328 --> 00:04:42.346
from Warren Buffett.

00:04:42.346 --> 00:04:45.221
- I've never met anybody
that spent the time to learn

00:04:45.221 --> 00:04:46.621
the game that didn't consider it

00:04:46.621 --> 00:04:48.214
one of the great things in their life.

00:04:48.214 --> 00:04:49.576
(numbers rattling)
(bell ringing)

00:04:49.576 --> 00:04:52.422
- Fact number five, he earned
money by delivering newspapers

00:04:52.422 --> 00:04:54.865
as a kid and he still loves doing it.

00:04:54.865 --> 00:04:57.698
- [Narrator] Buffett says
that from delivering papers,

00:04:57.698 --> 00:05:01.278
he learned the importance of
hard work and reliability.

00:05:01.278 --> 00:05:05.649
- And, I also learned, frankly,
that if you did a good job,

00:05:05.649 --> 00:05:07.566
you were going to move up.

00:05:09.090 --> 00:05:11.515
The very fact that I did a
good job in Spring Valley

00:05:11.515 --> 00:05:13.748
got me the Westchester routes later on.

00:05:13.748 --> 00:05:17.162
- [Narrator] Getting the
five-building Westchester Complex,

00:05:17.162 --> 00:05:20.612
home to Washington's
elite, transformed a job

00:05:20.612 --> 00:05:22.105
into a business.

00:05:22.105 --> 00:05:26.017
By the time World War II
ended, Buffett, not yet 15,

00:05:26.017 --> 00:05:30.613
had saved what an average
American earned in a year.

00:05:30.613 --> 00:05:34.679
- I was fortunate in that
I was here during probably

00:05:34.679 --> 00:05:36.711
the most interesting period
you could be in Washington

00:05:36.711 --> 00:05:37.695
during World War II.

00:05:37.695 --> 00:05:41.661
I mean, I delivered the
papers when Roosevelt died

00:05:41.661 --> 00:05:44.900
and when the atomic bomb was dropped.

00:05:44.900 --> 00:05:48.199
- [Narrator] Newspapers remain
close to Buffett's heart.

00:05:48.199 --> 00:05:51.610
He owns several and a
newspaper tossing contest

00:05:51.610 --> 00:05:54.379
is a regular event at
his Berkshire Hathaway

00:05:54.379 --> 00:05:55.771
annual meetings in Omaha.

00:05:55.771 --> 00:05:56.856
(crowd cheering)

00:05:56.856 --> 00:05:57.908
- I still like to deliver papers.

00:05:57.908 --> 00:05:59.788
I anybody's got an open
paper route out there,

00:05:59.788 --> 00:06:02.091
I'll be glad to help him out on it.

00:06:02.091 --> 00:06:03.459
(numbers rattling)
(bell ringing)

00:06:03.459 --> 00:06:07.867
- Fact number 6, he drinks
five Coca Colas every day.

00:06:07.867 --> 00:06:11.544
- I drink probably five
12-ounce Cokes a day.

00:06:11.544 --> 00:06:14.144
And, that's about 700 calories.

00:06:14.144 --> 00:06:18.227
And, I've been doing it
more or less all my life.

00:06:19.123 --> 00:06:22.511
I can't imagine anybody
that feels better than I do.

00:06:22.511 --> 00:06:25.178
I mean, I'm happy, I enjoy life.

00:06:26.190 --> 00:06:30.201
And, I've always wondered
if I'd had a twin,

00:06:30.201 --> 00:06:32.729
and he had to eat broccoli and asparagus

00:06:32.729 --> 00:06:34.386
and Brussels sprouts

00:06:34.386 --> 00:06:36.619
and done it every day and
I'd been drinking my Coke

00:06:36.619 --> 00:06:39.211
and eating potato chips
and there's some cookies

00:06:39.211 --> 00:06:42.378
that also have 100 calories per ounce,

00:06:44.512 --> 00:06:47.488
which of us, I think the
other guy would be gone.

00:06:47.488 --> 00:06:49.254
I mean, I think he probably

00:06:49.254 --> 00:06:53.024
would've committed suicide (chuckles).

00:06:53.024 --> 00:06:55.098
And, he certainly wouldn't feel
as good about life as I do.

00:06:55.098 --> 00:06:56.376
I feel good every day.

00:06:56.376 --> 00:06:57.647
(numbers rattling)
(bell ringing)

00:06:57.647 --> 00:06:59.048
- Fact number seven, he was awarded

00:06:59.048 --> 00:07:02.632
the Presidential Medal of
Freedom by Barack Obama.

00:07:02.632 --> 00:07:05.436
- [Host] As a world-renowned
investor and philanthropist,

00:07:05.436 --> 00:07:08.207
Warren E. Buffett's
business acumen is matched

00:07:08.207 --> 00:07:12.586
only by his dedication to
improving the lives of others.

00:07:12.586 --> 00:07:15.101
He is a co-founder of the Giving Pledge,

00:07:15.101 --> 00:07:17.590
an organization that
encourages wealthy Americans

00:07:17.590 --> 00:07:19.630
to donate at least 50% of their wealth

00:07:19.630 --> 00:07:21.630
to philanthropic causes.

00:07:22.611 --> 00:07:25.575
Warren Buffett's example of
generosity and compassion

00:07:25.575 --> 00:07:28.751
has shown us the power of one
individual's determination

00:07:28.751 --> 00:07:31.265
and inspired countless women
and men to help make our world

00:07:31.265 --> 00:07:32.682
a brighter place.

00:07:34.658 --> 00:07:37.575
(crowd applauding)

00:07:49.176 --> 00:07:50.571
(numbers rattling)
(bell ringing)

00:07:50.571 --> 00:07:52.846
- Fact number eight,
if you invested $10,000

00:07:52.846 --> 00:07:55.281
with Warren Buffett in 1956,

00:07:55.281 --> 00:07:58.364
it would be worth $500 million today.

00:07:59.288 --> 00:08:00.121
(upbeat piano music)

00:08:00.121 --> 00:08:03.658
- I formed my first
partnership in May of 1956.

00:08:03.658 --> 00:08:05.499
So, this was the year-end balance sheet,

00:08:05.499 --> 00:08:06.733
which I typed myself.

00:08:06.733 --> 00:08:08.522
In fact, you'd probably
find a few typos in it.

00:08:08.522 --> 00:08:11.414
And, these are my partners
I had at the time.

00:08:11.414 --> 00:08:12.984
And, this was my father-in-law.

00:08:12.984 --> 00:08:14.343
That was my roommate in college.

00:08:14.343 --> 00:08:15.448
That was his mother.

00:08:15.448 --> 00:08:16.654
That's my Aunt Alice.

00:08:16.654 --> 00:08:19.404
That's my sister, Doris, and
that's her husband, Truman,

00:08:19.404 --> 00:08:21.100
and that was the gang.
- And, you.

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:24.979
- If they kept the $10,000
investment and then

00:08:24.979 --> 00:08:27.468
when I liquidated the
partnership, reinvested that

00:08:27.468 --> 00:08:31.006
in Berkshire Hathaway, they would now have

00:08:31.006 --> 00:08:32.506
about 500 million.

00:08:35.219 --> 00:08:36.868
(numbers rattling)
(bell ringing)

00:08:36.868 --> 00:08:40.292
- And, fact number nine, he
loves playing the ukulele.

00:08:40.292 --> 00:08:43.365
- And, this is Warren Buffett singing

00:08:43.365 --> 00:08:45.255
Frank Sinatra's My Way.

00:08:45.255 --> 00:08:46.857
- Joined by Piers.

00:08:46.857 --> 00:08:48.018
- I will do a bit of
murmuring in the background.

00:08:48.018 --> 00:08:50.323
- Let's do it here now.

00:08:50.323 --> 00:08:54.534
♫ And, now the end is near

00:08:54.534 --> 00:08:58.411
♫ And, so I face the final curtain

00:08:58.411 --> 00:09:03.060
♫ My friends I'll say it clear

00:09:03.060 --> 00:09:07.227
♫ I'll say it something or
else of which I'm certain

00:09:08.203 --> 00:09:12.091
♫ I've lived a life that's full

00:09:12.091 --> 00:09:16.277
♫ And, traveled each and every highway

00:09:16.277 --> 00:09:20.002
♫ And more, much more than this

00:09:20.002 --> 00:09:22.835
♫ I did it my way

00:09:25.131 --> 00:09:26.402
- Fabulous (Piers claps)
- And, that's as far as we go.

00:09:26.402 --> 00:09:28.483
- That was absolutely fabulous.

00:09:28.483 --> 00:09:31.178
- That's a lot of talent.

00:09:31.178 --> 00:09:34.172
- And so, no, it's my
great pleasure and honor

00:09:34.172 --> 00:09:37.320
and I'm more nervous now than
I've been on stadium stages,

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:40.600
but Mr. Warren Buffett and I
are going to perform for you.

00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:43.687
- Thank you and this has been described

00:09:43.687 --> 00:09:45.357
as a once-in-a-lifetime experience

00:09:45.357 --> 00:09:46.455
and you'll understand why.

00:09:46.455 --> 00:09:49.215
Once will be enough.
(audience laughing)

00:09:49.215 --> 00:09:51.634
- Then again, once you
get a taste of these girls

00:09:51.634 --> 00:09:53.358
yelling for you, sir...
- Well, I'm sure of that.

00:09:53.358 --> 00:09:55.463
(audience laughing)

00:09:55.463 --> 00:09:56.309
Finally groupies.

00:09:56.309 --> 00:09:57.716
I've been waiting all my life.

00:09:57.716 --> 00:09:59.506
(audience laughing)

00:09:59.506 --> 00:10:02.338
Now pay particular
attention to the first line

00:10:02.338 --> 00:10:05.537
because this is what it's all about.

00:10:05.537 --> 00:10:09.866
♫ You've got to give a little

00:10:09.866 --> 00:10:12.144
♫ Take a little

00:10:12.144 --> 00:10:16.299
♫ And let your poor heart break a little

00:10:16.299 --> 00:10:18.236
♫ That's the story of

00:10:18.236 --> 00:10:21.819
♫ That's the glory of love

00:10:23.660 --> 00:10:26.656
♫ You got to laugh a little

00:10:26.656 --> 00:10:28.885
♫ Cry a little

00:10:28.885 --> 00:10:33.175
♫ And let the cloud roll by a little

00:10:33.175 --> 00:10:35.356
♫ That's the story of

00:10:35.356 --> 00:10:38.939
♫ That's the glory of love

00:10:41.116 --> 00:10:44.929
♫ As long as there's the two of us

00:10:44.929 --> 00:10:49.637
♫ We got the world and all its charms

00:10:49.637 --> 00:10:54.258
♫ When the world is through with us

00:10:54.258 --> 00:10:56.458
♫ We got each other's arms

00:10:56.458 --> 00:11:00.498
♫ You've got to win a little

00:11:00.498 --> 00:11:02.591
♫ Lose a little

00:11:02.591 --> 00:11:06.684
♫ And always have those blues a little

00:11:06.684 --> 00:11:08.707
♫ That's the story of

00:11:08.707 --> 00:11:12.290
♫ That's the glory of love

00:11:13.699 --> 00:11:14.532
- We did it.

00:11:14.532 --> 00:11:15.749
(audience cheering)

00:11:15.749 --> 00:11:17.108
- [John] Ladies and gentlemen.

00:11:17.108 --> 00:11:18.382
- So, thank you guys so much for watching.

00:11:18.382 --> 00:11:19.978
I hope you enjoyed this fun video.

00:11:19.978 --> 00:11:22.122
I'd love to know what did you guys think.

00:11:22.122 --> 00:11:23.630
Leave it down in the comments below.

00:11:23.630 --> 00:11:27.175
Which fact was the newest one
for you or did you not know

00:11:27.175 --> 00:11:30.724
or do you want to add a 10,
11, 12 to the list as well?

00:11:30.724 --> 00:11:32.876
Share you thoughts in the comments.

00:11:32.876 --> 00:11:34.455
Thank you guys again for watching.

00:11:34.455 --> 00:11:35.577
I believe in you.

00:11:35.577 --> 00:11:37.541
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:11:37.541 --> 00:11:40.138
and whatever your one word is.

00:11:40.138 --> 00:11:42.555
Much love, I'll see you soon.

